[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-04", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 04 — width, the catalog cache, and the hydrate regime", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-04.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 04 - all three seeds, and the two regimes finally separated\n\n> Seeds from iteration 03: measure the hydration chain; ask whether the catalog\n> is cacheable; find where `packs opened` stops saturating at 3.\n\n## A. `packs opened` tracks query WIDTH, not history\n\nIteration 03 saw 3 at every history length and said that was a property of that\ntree rather than a bound. It is neither: it is a function of how wide the query\nis.\n\n  commits  rare  blocks  rows  per-object  opened   N   N/P  3P+N     vs\n        8     3      82     3          12       3   6  2.00    15  0.80x\n        8    30      88    30          20       5  10  2.00    25  0.80x\n        8   300      88   300          32       6  16  2.67    34  0.94x\n        8   800      82   800          36       6  18  3.00    36  1.00x\n       32     3     270     3          12       3   6  2.00    15  0.80x\n       32    30     297    30          20       6  10  1.67    28  0.71x\n\nOpens rise 3 -> 5 -> 6 and then stop, while N keeps rising, so N/P climbs with\nwidth. At the widest query measured it reaches exactly 3.00 and exactly 1.00x --\nthe crossover `3P + N = 2N` predicted in iteration 03, hit on the nose.\n\nThe history axis does not move it: 8 commits and 32 commits give the same 3\nopens for the same narrow query. What iteration 03 read as saturation was the\nnarrow-query value.\n\n**So for QUERY workloads, packing never wins here. It reaches parity at the\nwidest query and loses everywhere else.**\n\n## B. The catalog IS cacheable, and that is a deployment question with a number\n\nTwo queries, one held catalog:\n\n    query      reads  catalog  total\n    1 (cold)       7        1      8\n    2 (warm)       7        0      7\n\nThe `+P` term is paid once per process, not once per query. A Worker that holds\nthe catalog gets the crossover back from `N/P > 3` to `N/P > 2` for every query\nafter its first. Iteration 03's rule is therefore the COLD rule; the warm rule\nis iteration 02's.\n\nNote what is not shared: the pack OPENS. Those are a separate cache and this\nbench does not hold them across queries either -- holding them too would remove\nthe `2P` term as well, and nothing here measures whether that is safe (a pack's\nindex is immutable, so it looks safe, and looking safe is not measuring it).\n\n## C. The hydrate shape is where packing wins — measured end to end\n\nReading every block of a snapshot one at a time, which is what a hydrate loop\ndoes and what `kotobase-storage-pack` records as 92% of a production query:\n\n    blocks  packs  per-object  opened  reads  catalog  total    N/P     vs\n        10      1          20       1     12        1     13  10.00  1.54x\n        18      2          36       2     22        2     24   9.00  1.50x\n        30      4          60       4     38        4     42   7.50  1.43x\n\nN/P is 7.5 to 10 -- far above the crossover on either rule -- and packing wins\n1.43x to 1.54x WITH the catalog priced. This is the first end-to-end measurement\nof that regime through this stack rather than through the store alone.\n\n## What the three sections say together\n\nThe two workloads are not close to each other, and the boundary between them is\nnot a matter of tuning:\n\n    query   (narrow, index-shaped)   N/P 1.67-3.00   packing 0.71x - 1.00x\n    hydrate (read everything)        N/P 7.50-10.0   packing 1.43x - 1.54x\n\nThere is no setting of the packing policy that serves both. That is what\nADR-2608160100 means by declaring the layout per backend rather than choosing a\ndefault, and it is now measured on both sides instead of one.\n\n## What this does NOT say\n\n  - Still nothing in production writes a pack.  [WITHDRAWN 2026-09-09, later the same day] The correction that stood here was itself wrong, and the original sentence above is RIGHT. It said `net-kotobase/engine` carries the pack namespaces and sets KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES=1 in its testnet env, and concluded pack writes were LIVE there. The first half is true and the conclusion does not follow: a source tree is not a deployment. Measured from outside with no credential -- backend.kotobase.net/_health answers 503 for three missing secrets (KOTOBASE_B2_KEY_ID, KOTOBASE_B2_APP_KEY, KOTOBA_SEED), and kotobase-cf-wasm-testnet.aozora.app/_health answers 200 with fields (canonical_cloudflare_independent, object_store) that exist only in net-kotobase/control-plane/kotobase-graph-database's worker, a tree with NO pack namespaces at all. Both trees declare an env.testnet with the same worker name, so a deploy from either replaces the other, and control-plane deployed last. So nothing in production or testnet writes or reads a pack, and these iterations are evidence for a plane that is still unwired. Root ADR-2608170300 carries the measurement.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 05\n\n  - Hold the pack opens, not just the catalog. If both caches persist, packed\n    cost falls to `N` and beats per-object's `2N` on every workload -- which\n    would be a large claim, so it needs the measurement and not the arithmetic.\n  - Section C's shape is right and its dependency structure is not. Walk a real\n    novelty chain.\n  - Everything so far measures reading. The write side -- what it costs to seal\n    a pack per commit -- has never been measured at all.\n"}]
